{
  "name": "Mat Terraces",
  "stage": "Vents",
  "summary": "A tall slope above a vent row. Rising water carries minerals up, and bands of microbial mat trap them in layers. Each band is a slow crawl or a gap to find; the gate waits at the top, under the light.",
  "pillars": [
    "Layers, not halos: mats slow diffusion, so minerals collect just under each band. Each terrace is a resource floor with its own ceiling.",
    "Vertical progress: the map is a climb from the hot vents to the lit gate room, which previews the next stage (towards light).",
    "Mats are soft walls: any cell can crawl through slowly, so a gap is a fast route and a crawl is a slow but safe one.",
    "UV shafts come down from the top through the upper terraces: the higher you go, the more you need the pigment gene."
  ],
  "proposals": [
    "Mat grazers eat mat tiles and open new gaps over time.",
    "Ceiling hunters: a predator that cruises the underside of a mat band, where prey gather."
  ],
